-- Q3179: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the VIE and omit all related disclosures","Consolidate the VIE using proportional consolidation methods","Disclose its maximum exposure to loss and its VIE involvement","Account for the VIE using the cost method on its books"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810, an entity with a variable interest in a VIE that is not the primary beneficiary must disclose its maximum exposure to loss, the nature and purpose of the VIE, and its involvement. This ensures financial statement users understand potential risks even without consolidation.
Wrong (A): Ignoring the VIE is not permitted; ASC 810 requires disclosure of involvement even when consolidation is not required.
Wrong (B): Proportional consolidation is not an option under U.S. GAAP for VIEs; the entity either consolidates fully or discloses.
Wrong (D): The cost method does not apply to VIE accounting; the entity must follow the disclosure requirements of ASC 810.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3179;

-- Q3183: Advanced Consolidations [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Eliminated entirely from consolidated income and never recognized","Eliminated only in the year of the intercompany equipment sale","Eliminated at sale and recognized over the asset''s remaining useful life","Recognized immediately as a gain in the consolidated income statement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810, unrealized profit on intercompany depreciable asset sales is eliminated at the time of sale, then gradually recognized in consolidated income as the buying entity depreciates the asset. The excess depreciation based on inflated cost is reversed each period, effectively recognizing the deferred gain over the remaining useful life.
Wrong (A): The gain is not permanently eliminated; it is recognized over the asset''s remaining life through depreciation adjustments.
Wrong (B): Elimination is not limited to the sale year; the gain is deferred and recognized over multiple periods as depreciation is recorded.
Wrong (D): Immediate recognition would overstate consolidated income; the gain must be deferred and recognized as the asset is depreciated.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3183;

-- Q3184: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reported as investment income on the consolidated income statement","Reported in other comprehensive income as an unrealized gain","Added directly to consolidated revenue as earned income","Replaced by the subsidiary''s actual revenues, expenses, and balances"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The equity method provides a one-line consolidation on the parent''s separate books. In full consolidation under ASC 810, the equity method entries (investment income and investment account) are reversed and replaced with the subsidiary''s actual line-by-line revenues, expenses, assets, and liabilities.
Wrong (A): Equity method income is eliminated in consolidation; it is not reported as a separate investment income line.
Wrong (B): Equity method income is not reclassified to OCI; it is entirely removed and replaced by detailed subsidiary data.
Wrong (C): The parent''s equity method income is not added to revenue; it is eliminated and the subsidiary''s own revenue lines are consolidated instead.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3184;

-- Q3187: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The full $200,000 is reported as a financing activity outflow","The full $200,000 is reported as an operating expense on the income statement","$80,000 is reported as NCI dividends; the $120,000 parent share is eliminated","No dividend activity is reported on the consolidated financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Of the $200,000 dividend, $120,000 (60%) goes to the parent and is eliminated as an intercompany transaction under ASC 810. The remaining $80,000 (40%) goes to noncontrolling shareholders and is reported as a financing activity outflow in the consolidated statement of cash flows.
Wrong (A): The full $200,000 cannot be reported because the parent''s 60% share ($120,000) is an intercompany transaction that must be eliminated.
Wrong (B): Dividends are distributions to owners, not operating expenses; they are never reported on the consolidated income statement.
Wrong (D): The NCI portion ($80,000) must be disclosed as a financing outflow; only the intercompany portion is eliminated.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3187;

-- Q3192: Derivatives and Hedging [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Exchanging principal amounts between two counterparties at inception","Purchasing options on interest rate futures for rate protection","Buying and selling foreign currency at the same time","Paying a fixed rate and receiving a floating rate, or vice versa"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 815-10-15-83, an interest rate swap is a derivative contract in which one party pays a fixed rate and receives a floating rate (such as SOFR), while the counterparty does the opposite. Only the net interest differential is exchanged; no principal changes hands.
Wrong (A): Principal amounts are not exchanged in a plain vanilla interest rate swap; only net interest payments are settled periodically.
Wrong (B): Options on interest rate futures are separate derivative instruments, not the structure of an interest rate swap.
Wrong (C): Simultaneous currency transactions describe foreign currency swaps or spot transactions, not interest rate swaps.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3192;

-- Q3196: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Current period earnings as a component of net income","As an adjustment to the hedged item''s carrying amount on the balance sheet","Directly in retained earnings without passing through net income","Other comprehensive income, reclassified to earnings when hedged item affects income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 815-30-35-3, the effective portion of a cash flow hedge gain or loss is recognized in OCI and accumulated in AOCI. It is reclassified to earnings in the period the hedged forecasted transaction affects earnings, ensuring proper matching. The ineffective portion is recognized immediately in current earnings.
Wrong (A): Only the ineffective portion goes to current earnings immediately; the effective portion is deferred in OCI.
Wrong (B): Adjusting the hedged item''s carrying amount applies to fair value hedges under ASC 815-25, not cash flow hedges.
Wrong (C): Cash flow hedge gains and losses do not bypass the income statement; they are reclassified from AOCI to earnings when the hedged transaction occurs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3196;

-- Q3197: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The derivative perfectly offsets every change in the hedged item''s value","The hedge''s fair value change does not exactly offset the hedged item change","The derivative expires on its originally scheduled maturity date","Market interest rates remain stable throughout the entire reporting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Hedge ineffectiveness arises when the change in fair value or cash flows of the hedging instrument does not perfectly offset the change in the hedged item. Under ASC 815, ineffectiveness must be recognized in current earnings. Common causes include basis differences, timing mismatches, and credit risk changes.
Wrong (A): A perfect offset is the definition of full hedge effectiveness, not ineffectiveness.
Wrong (C): A derivative expiring on its scheduled date is normal maturity, not a measure of hedge ineffectiveness.
Wrong (D): Stable interest rates would minimize both the hedged item change and hedge value change, not create ineffectiveness.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3197;

-- Q3203: Derivatives and Hedging [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The current fair value of the derivative instrument","The amount of cash exchanged between the two counterparties","A reference amount used to calculate payments, not the amount at risk","The maximum possible loss exposure on the derivative contract"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-10-15-83, the notional amount is a reference quantity (units, shares, or dollars) used to calculate periodic settlement payments. For an interest rate swap, payments equal the notional multiplied by the rate differential. The notional itself is never exchanged between parties.
Wrong (A): Fair value reflects the derivative''s market value based on rate changes, not the notional amount used for payment calculations.
Wrong (B): Only the net interest differential is exchanged, not the notional amount; actual cash flows are a small fraction of the notional.
Wrong (D): Maximum possible loss depends on market conditions and counterparty risk, not the notional amount, which is merely a calculation reference.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3203;

-- Q3204: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recorded in OCI and reclassified to earnings when the oil is consumed","Recognized immediately in current period earnings as a trading gain","Offset against the cost of inventory with no separate income recognition","Deferred and not recognized until the oil is sold to end customers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 815-30-35-3, if designated as a cash flow hedge, the effective gain on the futures contract is recorded in OCI. When the oil is purchased, the gain remains in AOCI. When the oil is consumed (recognized in COGS), the gain is reclassified from AOCI to earnings, matching the hedged transaction.
Wrong (B): Immediate earnings recognition applies only to derivatives not designated as hedges or to the ineffective portion of a hedge.
Wrong (C): The gain is tracked separately in AOCI and reclassified to earnings; it is not netted invisibly against inventory cost.
Wrong (D): Recognition occurs when the oil is consumed (COGS), not when it is resold; the hedged transaction is the purchase, not a downstream sale.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3204;

-- Q3207: Derivatives and Hedging [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A cash flow hedge of a forecasted foreign currency transaction","A net investment hedge of a foreign subsidiary operation","A fair value hedge of an unrecognized firm commitment","A speculative position with no hedge accounting designation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-20-25, a hedge of foreign currency risk in a firm commitment (a binding agreement) is classified as a fair value hedge. The firm commitment is recognized as an asset or liability at the change in exchange rate, with changes in the hedging derivative also recognized in earnings to create an offset.
Wrong (A): Cash flow hedges apply to forecasted transactions, not firm commitments; a binding agreement to purchase creates a firm commitment eligible for fair value hedge treatment.
Wrong (B): Net investment hedges apply to a net investment in a foreign operation, not to a purchase commitment for equipment.
Wrong (D): The forward was formally designated as a hedge of a firm commitment, so it qualifies for hedge accounting rather than speculative treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3207;

-- Q3210: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An interest rate swap with periodic net settlement payments","A forward currency contract for future foreign exchange delivery","A regular-way purchase or sale of a marketable security","A commodity futures contract traded on a regulated exchange"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 815-10-15-141, regular-way security purchases and sales (trades settling within the normal market delivery timeframe) are excluded from derivative accounting. They are accounted for on a trade-date or settlement-date basis under standard investment guidance.
Wrong (A): Interest rate swaps meet all derivative criteria under ASC 815, including notional amount, net settlement, and no initial net investment.
Wrong (B): Forward currency contracts are derivatives with a notional amount and future settlement, fully within the scope of ASC 815.
Wrong (D): Commodity futures contracts are exchange-traded derivatives that meet the ASC 815 definition and require derivative accounting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3210;

-- Q3211: Derivatives and Hedging [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The risk that the hedging strategy will be prohibitively expensive","The risk that the hedge and hedged item do not move in correlation","The risk that the counterparty will default on settlement obligations","The risk that interest rates will rise during the hedge period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Basis risk arises when the hedging instrument does not perfectly correlate with the hedged item. Under ASC 815, using crude oil futures to hedge jet fuel costs creates basis risk because crude oil and jet fuel prices may diverge, producing hedge ineffectiveness.
Wrong (A): The cost of establishing a hedge is a transaction cost concern, not basis risk; basis risk specifically refers to imperfect price correlation.
Wrong (C): Counterparty default risk is credit risk, a separate concept from basis risk which measures price correlation between hedge and hedged item.
Wrong (D): Rising interest rates describe directional market risk, not basis risk; basis risk is about the correlation gap between two related but different instruments.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3211;

-- Q3214: Derivatives and Hedging [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Off-balance-sheet with disclosure only in the financial footnotes","As a current or noncurrent asset at fair value on the balance sheet","As a direct reduction of stockholders'' equity on the balance sheet","At notional amount within the other assets line on the balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 815-10-35-1, all derivatives are recognized on the balance sheet at fair value. A derivative with a positive fair value is reported as an asset, classified as current or noncurrent based on the expected settlement or maturity date.
Wrong (A): Pre-ASC 815, some derivatives were off-balance-sheet, but current GAAP requires all derivatives to be recognized on the balance sheet at fair value.
Wrong (C): Derivatives are not reported as equity reductions; a derivative asset is reported among assets, while only certain hedge gains/losses flow through OCI within equity.
Wrong (D): Derivatives are measured at fair value, not notional amount; the notional is a reference for calculating payments, not the reported balance sheet value.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3214;

-- Q3221: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Property taxes levied on all real estate in the jurisdiction","Sales taxes collected on retail transactions citywide","Fees charged by the parks department for pool admission","Unrestricted state aid received for general government operations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 48, program revenues are directly associated with a specific function or program and include charges for services, operating grants, and capital grants. Swimming pool fees are charged by the parks and recreation function, qualifying as charges for services.
Wrong (A): Property taxes are general revenues because they are not tied to a specific program; they support governmental activities broadly.
Wrong (B): Sales taxes are general revenues under GASB 34; they are collected broadly and not restricted to any particular function or program.
Wrong (D): Unrestricted state aid is classified as general revenue because it is not linked to a specific government program or function.$EXPL$
