-- Q3223: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A proprietary fund used to account for enterprise activities","The chief operating fund for resources not reported in another fund","A fiduciary fund used to hold custodial resources for others","An internal service fund for charges between government departments"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 54, paragraph 30, the General Fund is the government''s chief operating fund and accounts for all financial resources not required to be reported in another fund. Every government must have exactly one General Fund, and it is always reported as a major fund.
Wrong (A): Proprietary funds (enterprise and internal service) account for business-type activities, not the government''s general operating resources.
Wrong (C): Fiduciary funds hold resources for external parties or in a custodial capacity, not for the government''s own general operations.
Wrong (D): Internal service funds are a type of proprietary fund for interdepartmental services, not the government''s chief operating fund.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3223;

-- Q3224: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Modified accrual with a current financial resources focus","Cash basis recognizing transactions only when cash is received","Full accrual with an economic resources measurement focus","Budgetary basis tied to the government''s adopted annual budget"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 93, proprietary funds (enterprise and internal service funds) use the full accrual basis of accounting with the economic resources measurement focus. Revenues are recognized when earned and expenses when incurred, similar to private-sector businesses.
Wrong (A): Modified accrual with the current financial resources focus applies to governmental funds (General, special revenue, etc.), not proprietary funds.
Wrong (B): Cash basis accounting is not an acceptable basis for proprietary fund reporting under GASB standards; accrual accounting is required.
Wrong (D): Budgetary basis is used for budgetary comparison schedules in governmental funds, not for proprietary fund financial reporting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3224;

-- Q3226: State and Local Government Reporting [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Account for the day-to-day general operations of the government","Account for resources restricted to acquiring or constructing capital facilities","Manage the government''s pooled cash and long-term investment portfolio","Account for employee pension benefits and other post-employment obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 65, capital projects funds account for financial resources restricted, committed, or assigned for the acquisition or construction of major capital facilities other than those financed by proprietary or trust funds.
Wrong (A): Day-to-day general operations are accounted for in the General Fund, not capital projects funds.
Wrong (C): Pooled cash and investment management is an administrative function, not the purpose of capital projects funds, which track construction-related resources.
Wrong (D): Pension benefits and post-employment obligations are reported through fiduciary funds (pension trust funds), not capital projects funds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3226;

-- Q3230: State and Local Government Reporting [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Depreciate infrastructure using the straight-line method over its useful life","Exclude all infrastructure assets from the government-wide financial statements","Report infrastructure assets at fair value with annual revaluation adjustments","Maintain an asset management system and conduct condition assessments every three years"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Statement No. 34, paragraph 23, the modified approach requires an asset management system documenting up-to-date condition, condition assessments at least every three years, and evidence that infrastructure is maintained at or above the established condition level. Maintenance costs are expensed as incurred rather than depreciated.
Wrong (A): Straight-line depreciation is the standard approach; the modified approach is an alternative that avoids depreciation by preserving asset condition.
Wrong (B): Infrastructure assets must be reported on the government-wide statements regardless of which approach is used; exclusion is not permitted under GASB 34.
Wrong (C): Fair value revaluation is not required; the modified approach preserves historical cost on the balance sheet and relies on condition assessments instead.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3230;

-- Q3232: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Account for resources restricted to specific purposes other than debt or capital","Account for scheduled principal and interest payments on general obligation bonds","Manage the government''s pooled cash and centralized investment portfolio","Record assets held in trust for employee pension benefits and retirees"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB Statement No. 54, paragraph 30, special revenue funds account for the proceeds of specific revenue sources that are restricted or committed to expenditure for specified purposes other than debt service or capital projects. A dedicated hotel tax restricted to tourism promotion fits this definition.
Wrong (B): Scheduled principal and interest payments on bonds are accounted for in debt service funds, not special revenue funds.
Wrong (C): Pooled cash and investment management is an administrative function; it does not define the purpose of special revenue funds.
Wrong (D): Assets held in trust for pension benefits are reported in fiduciary funds (pension trust funds), not in special revenue funds.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3232;

-- Q3236: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An operating lease expense recognized ratably over the lease term","A lease liability and a right-of-use intangible lease asset","No balance sheet recognition because the lease is an operating arrangement","Lease payments capitalized as governmental capital expenditures each year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 87, lessees recognize a lease liability (present value of payments) and an intangible right-of-use lease asset. The asset is amortized over the shorter of the lease term or useful life. Short-term leases of 12 months or less are excluded from this requirement.
Wrong (A): GASB 87 eliminated the operating lease classification for lessees; virtually all leases are now recognized on the balance sheet.
Wrong (C): Under GASB 87, leases require balance sheet recognition as both a liability and an intangible asset; off-balance-sheet treatment is no longer permitted.
Wrong (D): Lease payments are not treated as capital expenditures; the lessee records a right-of-use asset and amortizes it systematically.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3236;

-- Q3238: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Restricted to the government''s own programs with principal maintained intact","Available for any government purpose without spending restrictions","Held in trust for pension benefits of government employees and retirees","Restricted exclusively to debt service payments on outstanding obligations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34, permanent funds account for resources legally restricted so that only earnings (not principal) may be used to support government programs. The principal must be maintained intact in perpetuity. If the beneficiary is external parties rather than the government, a private-purpose trust fund is used instead.
Wrong (B): Permanent fund resources are not available for any purpose; the principal is legally restricted and only investment earnings may be spent on designated programs.
Wrong (C): Pension benefits are reported through fiduciary funds (pension trust funds), not permanent funds which support the government''s own programs.
Wrong (D): Debt service payments are accounted for in debt service funds; permanent funds are restricted to program purposes with principal preservation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3238;

-- Q3244: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["In a separate column alongside the primary government in government-wide statements","As a subsidiary using private-sector consolidation methods and procedures","Only in the notes to the financial statements with no fund-level reporting","As if they are part of the primary government within its fund statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Statement No. 14, blended component units are so closely related to the primary government that their funds are presented as if they are part of the primary government. Blending is required when the component unit''s governing body is substantively the same as the primary government or when the unit provides services exclusively to it.
Wrong (A): A separate column in the government-wide statements is the presentation method for discretely presented component units, not blended ones.
Wrong (B): Private-sector consolidation methods do not apply to governmental reporting; GASB uses blending and discrete presentation, not subsidiary consolidation.
Wrong (C): Note-only disclosure does not satisfy the reporting requirements for component units that meet the criteria for blending under GASB 14.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3244;

-- Q3245: State and Local Government Reporting [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Current period expenses that reduce net position immediately","Long-term investments expected to generate future returns for the government","Revenue earned in the current period but not yet collected in cash","Consumption of net assets that is applicable to a future reporting period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB Concepts Statement No. 4, deferred outflows of resources represent consumption of net assets applicable to a future reporting period. Common examples include deferred amounts on debt refunding and pension-related deferred outflows such as differences between projected and actual earnings.
Wrong (A): Deferred outflows are not current period expenses; they are specifically deferred because they relate to future periods rather than the current one.
Wrong (B): Deferred outflows are a distinct financial statement element from assets; they do not represent investments or future return-generating holdings.
Wrong (C): Revenue earned but not collected describes a receivable (an asset), not a deferred outflow of resources which represents a consumption of net assets.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3245;

-- Q3248: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only external direct costs of materials and purchased services","No costs are capitalized during the application development stage","All overhead costs plus direct costs including general and administrative","External direct costs and payroll for employees devoted to the project"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 350-40-25-2, costs incurred during the application development stage are capitalized, including external direct costs of materials and services, payroll costs for employees directly devoted to the project, and interest costs. These costs directly contribute to creating the asset''s future economic benefit.
Wrong (A): External direct costs alone are incomplete; internal payroll for employees directly working on the project is also capitalizable under ASC 350-40.
Wrong (B): The application development stage is specifically when capitalization begins; it is the preliminary project stage and post-implementation stage where costs are expensed.
Wrong (C): General overhead and administrative costs are not capitalizable; only costs directly attributable to the development effort qualify under ASC 350-40.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3248;

-- Q3250: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Aldridge capitalizes $600,000 as an intangible; Bancroft expenses $600,000 immediately as an operating cost","Aldridge expenses ratably as service cost with implementation costs as prepaid; Bancroft capitalizes as software intangible under ASC 350-40","Both produce identical financial statement effects because the software platform is the same","Neither creates any asset because cloud computing never qualifies for capitalization under GAAP"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2018-15, Aldridge''s service contract is a hosting arrangement where the $600,000 is recognized as service expense over the term, with qualifying implementation costs capitalized as a prepaid asset. Bancroft''s software license is capitalized as an intangible under ASC 350-40 and amortized over its useful life, creating a larger initial balance sheet asset. Cash outflows are operating for Aldridge and investing for Bancroft.
Wrong (A): This reverses the treatment; service contracts do not create intangible assets, and software licenses are capitalized rather than expensed immediately.
Wrong (C): Classification as a service contract versus a software license produces materially different accounting treatment despite identical software.
Wrong (D): Both ASC 350-40 (software licenses) and ASU 2018-15 (service contract implementation costs) permit capitalization of qualifying costs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3250;

-- Q3254: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An intangible asset on the balance sheet under ASC 350-40","A prepaid expense classified as current or noncurrent by hosting term","Property, plant, and equipment alongside tangible fixed assets","A right-of-use asset similar to lease accounting treatment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40-25-16A), implementation costs for a cloud computing arrangement classified as a service contract are capitalized and presented as a prepaid asset. Classification as current or noncurrent is based on the remaining hosting arrangement term.
Wrong (A): Intangible asset classification applies to internal-use software developed in-house under ASC 350-40; a service contract does not convey software control to the customer.
Wrong (C): PP&E is for tangible assets; capitalized CCA implementation costs are not physical assets and do not belong in this category.
Wrong (D): Right-of-use assets arise under lease accounting (ASC 842 or GASB 87); CCA service contracts are not leases and do not create right-of-use assets.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3254;

-- Q3255: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always capitalized as part of the software development project cost","Capitalized only for software-related activities; data purging costs are expensed","Always expensed as incurred regardless of the development stage","Deferred as a separate asset until the software becomes fully operational"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-40, data conversion costs are split. Costs directly associated with making old data compatible with the new software (coding and testing conversion routines) may be capitalized during the application development stage. However, costs for purging or cleansing existing data are expensed as incurred.
Wrong (A): Not all data conversion costs are capitalizable; only software-related conversion activities qualify, while data cleansing and purging are expensed.
Wrong (C): Blanket expensing is incorrect because the software-related portion of data conversion (coding conversion routines) qualifies for capitalization under ASC 350-40.
Wrong (D): There is no deferral concept for data conversion costs; qualifying costs are capitalized as incurred during the application development stage, not deferred until go-live.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3255;

-- Q3256: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expensed as incurred because they are post-implementation maintenance costs","Recorded as a separate intangible asset apart from the existing ERP system","Capitalized if the upgrade provides additional functionality beyond original capability","Charged as a reduction against the original capitalized software cost basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-40-25-7, costs of upgrades and enhancements to internal-use software that result in additional functionality should be capitalized. The critical test is whether the modification enables the software to perform tasks it could not previously perform, which a new analytics module satisfies.
Wrong (A): Post-implementation costs for routine maintenance and bug fixes are expensed, but upgrades providing significant new functionality are capitalized under ASC 350-40.
Wrong (B): Enhancement costs are typically added to the existing software asset''s carrying amount, not recorded as a separate intangible asset.
Wrong (D): Upgrade costs are added to the asset''s carrying value, not charged against or netted from the original cost basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3256;

-- Q3259: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Expensed immediately as a period cost in the current year","Recorded as a lease liability under ASC 842 lease guidance","Capitalized as a prepaid asset and amortized over the 3-year term","Expensed ratably over the 3-year contract as subscription cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40), implementation costs incurred during the application development stage of a hosting arrangement classified as a service contract are capitalized as a prepaid asset. The $100,000 is amortized over the 3-year hosting term, while the $5,000 monthly subscription fees are expensed as incurred.
Wrong (A): Immediate expensing applies to preliminary project stage costs, not application development stage implementation costs which qualify for capitalization.
Wrong (B): SaaS arrangements classified as service contracts are not leases; they do not create lease liabilities under ASC 842.
Wrong (D): Ratable expensing over the contract term applies to subscription fees, not to capitalizable implementation costs incurred during the application development stage.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3259;

-- Q3260: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["In the same income statement line item as the ongoing hosting fees","As depreciation expense within operating costs on the income statement","As a separate line item for cloud computing amortization expense","As an investing activity outflow on the statement of cash flows"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASU 2018-15 (codified in ASC 350-40-35-8A), amortization of capitalized implementation costs for a hosting arrangement classified as a service contract is presented in the same income statement line item as the ongoing hosting fees, typically within SG&A or cost of services.
Wrong (B): Depreciation expense applies to tangible assets; capitalized CCA implementation costs follow the hosting arrangement''s service expense classification, not depreciation.
Wrong (C): A separate line item is not required or appropriate; ASC 350-40-35-8A specifically requires presentation alongside the hosting fees for consistency.
Wrong (D): Cash flow classification as investing applies to internal-use software capitalized under ASC 350-40, not to service contract implementation costs which are classified as operating.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3260;
